    {
      "id": "ac_final_check",
      "type": "check",
      "kind": "understanding",
      "title": "בדיקת סוף",
      "body_he": "שאלה אחת על כל הדרך.",
      "body_content": "The small library on Herzl Street used to close at noon. Last winter the city kept it open until eight, after a group of students wrote a letter to the mayor. They said the afternoon was the only time they could study, because the school building locked its doors at two. The mayor agreed, but she asked for a plan. The students offered to shelve books and to sit with younger children during homework hour. A retired teacher, Mr. Cohen, joined them on Mondays. He does not run the desk. He listens, and he asks one question before he gives an answer. Not everyone was pleased. Two shop owners said the evening light made the street noisy. The library staff counted the visitors for a month. On school nights the room was full. On Fridays it was quiet. The city did not add money for new books, so the students collected used ones from their neighbours. The rule is simple: a book can leave for two weeks, and a dictionary stays in the room. Maya, who works nearby, comes after four and reads the paper. She says the library is not a school and not a shop. It is the one room on the street where nobody asks her to hurry.\n\nWhich sentence is correct English about the text?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "label": "Last winter the city kept the library open until eight."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "label": "Last winter the city has kept library open until eight."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "label": "The last winter a city kept the library open until eight."
        }
